Day 34 – OpenFeign Client (Spring Cloud)

OpenFeign is a declarative REST client that simplifies communication between microservices in a Spring Boot ecosystem.
Instead of writing RestTemplate or WebClient code, you just define an interface and OpenFeign handles the REST calls automatically.

OpenFeign is part of Spring Cloud OpenFeign and works smoothly with Netflix Eureka for service discovery.

1️⃣ Add OpenFeign Dependency

Add the dependency in pom.xml:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-openfeign</artifactId>
</dependency>

Also ensure Spring Cloud dependency management is configured.

2️⃣ Enable Feign Client

In your main Spring Boot class, enable Feign clients.

import org.springframework.cloud.openfeign.EnableFeignClients;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableFeignClients
public class OrderServiceApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(OrderServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}

This tells Spring Boot to scan for Feign interfaces.

3️⃣ Create Feign Client Interface

Example: Order Service calling Product Service.

import org.springframework.cloud.openfeign.FeignClient;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;

@FeignClient(name = "product-service")
public interface ProductClient {

    @GetMapping("/products/{id}")
    Product getProductById(@PathVariable Long id);
}
Explanation

@FeignClient(name="product-service")

The name must match the service name registered in Eureka.

Feign automatically resolves the service location.

4️⃣ Use the Feign Client in a Service
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final ProductClient productClient;

    public OrderService(ProductClient productClient) {
        this.productClient = productClient;
    }

    public Product getProduct(Long id) {
        return productClient.getProductById(id);
    }
}

Now Order Service → calls → Product Service automatically.

5️⃣ Example Controller
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
public class OrderController {

    private final OrderService orderService;

    public OrderController(OrderService orderService) {
        this.orderService = orderService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/product/{id}")
    public Product getProduct(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return orderService.getProduct(id);
    }
}
6️⃣ Flow of Communication
Client Request
     ↓
Order Service
     ↓
Feign Client
     ↓
Product Service (via Eureka)
     ↓
Response returned
7️⃣ Advantages of OpenFeign

✅ Less boilerplate code
✅ Easy microservice communication
✅ Integrates with Eureka
✅ Supports load balancing
✅ Supports fallback with Resilience4j

